# Implementing the isMountain Method

Looking at the problem, I need to implement a method that checks if an array has the "mountain property" - meaning elements increase until a peak value, then decrease.

## Understanding the Requirements

According to the problem description, an array has the mountain property if:
- Elements increase until a maximum value (peak)
- Elements decrease after the peak
- There must be a peak (not just all increasing or all decreasing)

## Solution Approach

The Mountain class already provides some useful methods:
- `isIncreasing(int[] array, int stop)`: Checks if elements from index 0 to stop are strictly increasing
- `isDecreasing(int[] array, int start)`: Checks if elements from index start to the end are strictly decreasing
- `getPeakIndex(int[] array)`: Returns the index of the peak (local maximum)

Here's how I would implement the `isMountain` method:

```java
public static boolean isMountain(int[] array) {
    // Check if the array has enough elements
    if (array.length < 3) {
        return false; // Need at least 3 elements to have a peak
    }

    // Find the peak index
    int peakIndex = getPeakIndex(array);

    // Make sure a valid peak exists
    if (peakIndex <= 0 || peakIndex >= array.length - 1) {
        return false; // Peak cannot be at first or last index
    }

    // Check if elements before peak are increasing
    // and elements after peak are decreasing
    return isIncreasing(array, peakIndex) && isDecreasing(array, peakIndex);
}
```

This implementation checks if:
1. The array has at least 3 elements (minimum needed for a mountain)
2. There's a valid peak (not at the edges)
3. The array increases up to the peak and decreases after the peak

The solution correctly handles all the test cases shown in the example.

